// The case tables — three constant maps, spelled today as six arrays and a binary search.
//
// `packages/unicode/src/tables.wac` is **generated**: *"Generated by tools/gentables.ts. Do not
// edit."* 65 KB of literals holding the code points whose mapping differs from themselves, about
// 2,909 of them against 1.1 million, as
//
//     const i32[] LOWER_KEYS   = i32[](65, 66, 67, …);   // 1459
//     const i32[] LOWER_VALUES = i32[](97, 98, 99, …);   // 1459
//     const i32[] UPPER_KEYS   …  UPPER_VALUES   …       // 1450 each
//     const i32[] FOLD_KEYS    …  FOLD_VALUES    …       // 1481 each
//
// *"A lookup is a binary search over KEYS and an index into VALUES."*
//
// **Three maps, six arrays, and the pairing is a parameter.** The search is written once — the
// shipped file has one `lookup(i32[] keys, i32[] values, i32 cp)` and three one-line callers — and
// that is the sharper version of the problem rather than a mitigation of it:
//
//     export i32 simpleLower(i32 cp) { return lookup(LOWER_KEYS, LOWER_VALUES, cp); }
//     export i32 simpleUpper(i32 cp) { return lookup(UPPER_KEYS, UPPER_VALUES, cp); }
//     export i32 simpleFold(i32 cp)  { return lookup(FOLD_KEYS,  FOLD_VALUES,  cp); }
//
// `lookup` takes **two arrays that must be the same length in the same order**, six arrays make three
// such pairs, and nothing checks that a call passes a matching one.
// `lookup(LOWER_KEYS, UPPER_VALUES, cp)` compiles and answers plausible nonsense — no bound is
// violated, because the arrays are within one of each other in length.
//
// That is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *paired values with nothing holding the pair* at the largest scale
// in the repository — 4,390 pairs across three tables — and the first time the pair is passed
// **through a function signature** rather than held in a struct. A `Table` makes the pairing
// unspellable-wrongly: there is one argument and it is the table.

import { Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * One constant lookup, keyed by code point.
 *
 * `Entry[]` rather than two `i32[]`, which is the change and is measured rather than argued:
 * `../../../bench/dispatchcost.wac` says an array of records is **12% faster to read** than the same
 * data in parallel lanes, because wasm bounds-checks every index and a struct field is an unchecked
 * offset. A binary search probes the key array once per step and the value array once at the end —
 * so lanes pay a check per probe and records pay one.
 *
 * The build side of that bench says the opposite, and the crossover is about fifteen reads per
 * element. **A generated table is built once per module and read for the life of the program**, so
 * it is on the far side of the crossover by any margin anyone would care about.
 */
export struct Entry { i32 from; i32 to; }

/** The three mappings, so a caller names one rather than picking a pair of arrays. */
export enum Mapping { Lower, Upper, Fold }

/**
 * The table for one mapping, sorted by `from`.
 *
 * `Table` rather than a bare `Entry[]` because *sorted by `from`* is the precondition every lookup
 * depends on and no signature carries. Same shape as `../../../core/order.wac`'s `uniqueByKey`, which
 * takes a `Vec` that must already be in order and cannot say so, and the same non-answer: a
 * constructor that sorts is a constructor a generated file does not want to run.
 */
export struct Table {
  Entry[] entries;

  /** The mapped code point, or `cp` unchanged — the tables hold only what differs. */
  i32 map(const this, i32 cp) { … }
}

export Table table(Mapping m) { … }

// ## 1. What a constant map costs today, counted
//
// Three files in `packages/` carry the header *"Generated by"*, and **all three are parallel key and
// value arrays with a hand-written binary search**:
//
//     packages/unicode/src/tables.wac      LOWER, UPPER, FOLD          6 arrays, 4,390 pairs
//     packages/unicode/src/printable.wac   PRINT_KEYS / PRINT_VALUES   2 arrays,   733 pairs
//     packages/raster/src/font16.wac       GLYPH_CP / GLYPH_AT / …     5 arrays, 3,240 glyphs
//
// **Five logical constant maps, eleven arrays, and a binary search written at each.** The largest
// single literal in the repository is `font16.wac`'s `GLYPH_ROWS0` at 8,192 elements.
//
// None of them can be a `Map<i32, i32>`, and the reason is not that `Map` is missing. It is that a
// `Map` is **built**, and a table with 1,459 entries built by inserting 1,459 times at module start
// is work a constant does not need. What is wanted is a map whose contents are known at compile time
// — and `../../../QUESTIONS.md` records the nearest thing anyone has asked for, *`Map<K, V>` with a
// compiler-supplied hash for payload-free enums*, which is a different question about a different
// key.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **A constant of a struct type.** `const i32[] KEYS = i32[](…)` is legal and
// `const Entry[] LOWER = Entry[](Entry(65, 97), …)` is the form this file wants, and nothing here has
// established whether it is legal. The array literal is; whether a `const` may hold references to
// 1,459 heap objects, and when they are built, is a question about module initialisation that
// `spec/` does not answer and this directory cannot run.
//
// **And the generator is the caller nobody consulted.** `tools/gentables.ts` writes the shape, so
// changing it is a change to a TypeScript program that emits wac — the only place in this exercise
// where the author of the code is a program. `../../../README.md`'s caller test asks *name a caller
// and say what it would do differently*; here the caller is a generator and it would do exactly what
// it is told, which makes the test vacuous and the change free. **A generated file is the one place
// a representation can be changed without asking anyone**, and it is where three of the largest
// parallel-array cases in the repository live.
//
// **`printable.wac` is a range table and not a map**, which is worth separating. Its pairs are *(start
// of range, value for the range)* and the lookup is *the last range starting at or before `cp`* —
// a different query, and `Entry { from; to; }` names it badly. A third type, or a `Range` beside
// this one; either way the two generated files want two shapes and today they have one.
